            <div xml:id="d38">

                <pb facs="0078" n="68" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg68"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 P.W./3–1245: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Atcheson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-03-12T10:00:00+07:00">March 12, 1945—10
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 15—7 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>411. French Ambassador told me last night he had information that Langson had
                    been captured by Japs, that there was street fighting progressing in Hanoi, and
                    that in Moncay a French garrison of 1,000 with 20 officers was still holding
                    out. (ReEmbs 403, March 11, 11 a.m.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d38-f1"/>) He said he
                    did not yet know whether any French troops had endeavored to cross into China.
                    Some time ago he had approached the Generalissimo with regard to that
                    possibility and Chiang had said that French troops would be welcomed and
                    assisted. Ho Ying-chin has recently made a similar statement, but he himself was
                    doubtful whether any definite plans for receiving French refugee soldiers in
                    China had been worked out or would prove very effective.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Atcheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
